codex
Step 0 supports a native family, not the initially suggested “sequence replay” family. The paper’s Section 3 explicitly searches over one of three pairings for every four-tile group; for isolated height-three stacks, a chosen global pairing is valid exactly when its top-before-bottom precedence graph is acyclic. That pairing assignment is a compact, exact witness, whereas a full removal sequence would make escalation grow the answer.

The paper also rules out stacks of heights one and two: Theorem 5 makes those polynomial and always solvable in the initial four-copy setting. I’m therefore testing only the NP-complete regime of Theorem 3—isolated `aab`/`abb` height-three stacks—and benchmarking the paper’s group-pairing recursion before committing to Track A.
